L["sermon"] = """Large crowds were following Jesus. They came from Galilee, the Decapolis, Jerusalem, Judea and the region across the Jordan. Many were sick, and he had been healing them. When Jesus saw the crowds, he went up on a mountainside and sat down. His disciples came to him, and he began to teach them. This became the most famous sermon ever preached.

It begins with the Beatitudes. Blessed are the poor in spirit, for theirs is the kingdom of heaven. Blessed are those who mourn, for they will be comforted. Blessed are the meek, for they will inherit the earth. Blessed are those who hunger and thirst for righteousness, for they will be filled. Blessed are the merciful, for they will be shown mercy. Blessed are the pure in heart, for they will see God. Blessed are the peacemakers, for they will be called children of God. Blessed are those who are persecuted because of righteousness, for theirs is the kingdom of heaven.

These were surprising. The world says that the blessed are the rich, the strong and the powerful. Jesus said that the blessed are the people who know they need God.

He told his followers that they were the salt of the earth and the light of the world. A city on a hill cannot be hidden. Let your light shine before others, that they may see your good deeds and glorify your Father in heaven.

Then he raised the bar. You have heard that it was said, do not murder. But I tell you that anyone who is angry with a brother or sister will be subject to judgment. You have heard that it was said, do not commit adultery. But I tell you that anyone who looks at someone lustfully has already committed adultery in his heart. Jesus was showing that God cares about the heart, not just outward behavior.

He taught, you have heard that it was said, love your neighbor and hate your enemy. But I tell you, love your enemies and pray for those who persecute you, that you may be children of your Father in heaven. If someone slaps you on the right cheek, turn to them the other cheek also.

He taught them to pray. Our Father in heaven, hallowed be your name, your kingdom come, your will be done, on earth as it is in heaven. Give us today our daily bread. And forgive us our debts, as we also have forgiven our debtors. And lead us not into temptation, but deliver us from the evil one.

He warned against worry. Do not worry about your life, what you will eat or drink. Look at the birds of the air. They do not sow or reap or store away in barns, and yet your heavenly Father feeds them. Are you not much more valuable than they? Seek first his kingdom and his righteousness, and all these things will be given to you as well.

He ended with a short story. Everyone who hears these words of mine and puts them into practice is like a wise man who built his house on the rock. The rain came down, the streams rose, and the winds blew and beat against that house, yet it did not fall. But everyone who hears these words and does not put them into practice is like a foolish man who built his house on sand. When the storm came, it fell with a great crash. When Jesus had finished, the crowds were amazed at his teaching, because he taught as one who had authority.

Why does this matter? The Sermon on the Mount describes what life in God's kingdom looks like. It isn't a list of rules to earn God's favor. It describes people who have been changed from the inside.

What happened after? Jesus came down from the mountain and began healing, including a man with leprosy. The crowds kept following. Over the centuries, this sermon has shaped believers and others, from the early church to Gandhi and Martin Luther King Jr.

What does this mean for us today? The sermon is both challenging and comforting. It shows us that we can't live this way on our own strength, and it points us to God's grace. It also reminds us that hearing isn't enough. The question is whether we build our lives on it. And it frees us from worry, because we have a Father who knows what we need.

That is why this story is so important. It says that the kingdom of God belongs to the humble, and that Jesus teaches with the authority of God himself."""
